        heading: "Formation, aging, and the electrical double-hit",
        body: "After assembly, every cell goes through formation — charged, discharged, and cycled for days to stabilize the chemistry. Formation is the plant's strangest load: it consumes enormous electrical energy while rejecting enormous heat, so it shows up twice — once as electrical demand, once as cooling load that becomes more electrical demand. Formation halls need precision cooling, robust power quality (a sag can scrap in-process cells), and fire detection tuned to the thermal signature of cycling cells. The formation profile also shapes the utility interconnection: it's a flat, relentless 24/7 load, which utilities actually prefer — if they know about it early.",
